//! # TKET2: The Hardware Agnostic Quantum Compiler
//!
//! TKET2 is an open source quantum compiler developed by Quantinuum. Central to
//! TKET2's design is its hardware agnosticism, which allows researchers and
//! quantum software developers to take advantage of its state of the art
//! compilation for many different quantum architectures.
//!
//! Circuits are represented as [HUGR][] programs. This crate provides a set of
//! tools to build, inspect, rewrite and optimise quantum circuits, and to
//! interoperate with the TKET1 JSON serialization format.
//!
//! ## Circuits
//!
//! The [`Circuit`] trait defines a common interface over any HUGR structure
//! that describes a circuit. It provides methods to query the circuit's
//! signature, qubit and gate counts, and to iterate over its gates as
//! [`Command`]s (an operation applied to a specific set of wires).
//!
//! The [`Hugr`] type from HUGR is the concretely most commonly used circuit
//! representation.
//!
//! ## Operations
//!
//! Common quantum operations are defined in the [`Tk2Op`] enum, covering
//! single-qubit and multi-qubit gates as well as measurement, reset and qubit
//! allocation and deallocation. When read from a serialized TKET1 circuit,
//! operations that have no direct representation are embedded as opaque
//! operations from the TKET1 extension.
//!
//! [`op_matches`] can be used to check whether an [`OpType`][hugr::ops::OpType]
//! corresponds to a given TKET2 operation.
//!
//! ## Rewriting
//!
//! TKET2's circuit optimisation is based on rewriting subcircuits by equivalent
//! ones:
//!
//! - A part of a circuit can be extracted as a [`Subcircuit`], and used to
//!   define a [`CircuitRewrite`] that replaces it with another circuit.
//! - Different [`Rewriter`]s search a circuit for the rewrite rules it matches.
//! - Rewrites are applied according to a
